{
  "concept": "The logistic sigmoid squashes any real input z into the open interval (0,1), rising monotonically through 0.5 at z = 0, which lets a real-valued score be read as a probability.",
  "figureType": "line_plot",
  "elements": [
    "Horizontal axis labeled 'z' with ticks at -5.0, 0.0, 5.0 and minor tick marks along the axis",
    "Vertical axis labeled 'sig[z]' with ticks at 0.0 and 1.0 and minor tick marks",
    "Black rectangular plot frame (box) enclosing the plotting area",
    "Smooth orange S-shaped sigmoid curve, thick stroke, from (-5, ~0.007) to (5, ~0.993)",
    "Formula annotation inside the plot, upper-centre: fraction 1 / (1 + exp[-z])",
    "Light gray dotted horizontal guide line at sig[z] = 0.5 running from the left axis to the curve",
    "Light gray dotted vertical guide line at z = 0 running from the x-axis up to the curve",
    "Draggable marker dot on the curve with a coordinate readout label (z, sig[z])",
    "Adjustable horizontal threshold line with its label (e.g. 'threshold = 0.50')",
    "Marker and dropped vertical line at the z value where the sigmoid crosses the threshold, labeled with the decision boundary z*",
    "Optional shaded regions left/right of the decision boundary indicating class 0 vs class 1"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "dragPointOnCurve",
      "type": "drag",
      "teaches": "That every real input z maps to a value strictly between 0 and 1, with the output changing fastest near z = 0 and barely at all in the saturated tails."
    },
    {
      "id": "threshold",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Probability threshold",
      "range": [0.05, 0.95, 0.01],
      "default": 0.5,
      "teaches": "That choosing a probability cut-off corresponds to a single decision boundary in z, and that the boundary moves nonlinearly — small threshold changes near 0.5 shift z a little, while thresholds near 0 or 1 push z far out into the tails."
    },
    {
      "id": "viewMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Overlay",
      "options": ["Sigmoid only", "Sigmoid + slope", "Decision regions"],
      "default": "Sigmoid only",
      "teaches": "That the derivative sig[z](1 - sig[z]) peaks at z = 0 and vanishes in the tails, which is exactly why saturated inputs stop learning — and how the curve partitions the z-axis into two predicted classes."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The S-curve",
      "narration": "The orange curve maps every real number z onto a value between 0 and 1. Drag the point along the curve: no matter how far out you go, the output never quite reaches 0 or 1.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Sigmoid only", "threshold": 0.5 }
    },
    {
      "title": "The midpoint at z = 0",
      "narration": "The dotted guides meet where sig[0] = 0.5 exactly. This is the curve's centre of symmetry and its steepest point — a zero score means complete uncertainty between the two classes.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Sigmoid only", "threshold": 0.5 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Slope and saturation",
      "narration": "Overlay the slope: it is largest at z = 0 and collapses toward zero in both tails. Drag the point out to z = ±4 and watch how little the output moves — this flatness is the vanishing-gradient problem.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Sigmoid + slope", "threshold": 0.5 }
    },
    {
      "title": "Choosing a threshold",
      "narration": "Set the probability threshold to 0.5 and the decision boundary sits at z = 0. The horizontal line crosses the curve at exactly one place, because the sigmoid is strictly increasing.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Decision regions", "threshold": 0.5 }
    },
    {
      "title": "A conservative threshold",
      "narration": "Raise the threshold to 0.9 and the boundary jumps out to z ≈ 2.2 — demanding high confidence costs a large margin in z. Push it to 0.99 and the boundary runs off toward the edge of the plot.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Decision regions", "threshold": 0.9 }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The sigmoid is a one-to-one, monotonic bridge between unbounded scores and bounded probabilities: every threshold on the probability axis corresponds to exactly one cut point on the z axis, but the mapping is steep in the middle and nearly flat in the tails.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Clean, textbook-style line plot with a heavy black box frame on white. X-axis spans -5 to 5 labeled 'z' with numeric ticks only at -5.0, 0.0, 5.0; y-axis spans 0 to 1 labeled 'sig[z]' (rotated) with ticks only at 0.0 and 1.0; small minor tick marks along both axes. Single thick salmon/terracotta orange curve (#D9724C-ish) with no markers, drawn smoothly. Upper-centre of the plot carries the serif math annotation 1/(1+exp[-z]) as a stacked fraction. Faint gray dotted guide lines: horizontal from the y-axis to the curve at height 0.5, and vertical from the x-axis to the curve at z = 0. The draggable point is a filled dark dot on the curve with a small boxed readout showing z and sig[z] to two decimals. The threshold line is a dashed blue/gray horizontal line across the full width with a matching dashed vertical drop at its crossing point, labeled z*. In 'Decision regions' mode, shade the region left of z* in a pale blue (class 0) and right in pale orange (class 1). In 'Sigmoid + slope' mode add a thinner secondary curve for sig[z](1-sig[z]) peaking at 0.25, drawn in a muted purple, clearly distinguished in a small legend."
}

